.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_DISABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_disable_sending \- turn off sends on a dynamic queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_disable_sending(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function marks the queue as closed to new sends. Every later send
returns ccol_not_permitted. This continues until a call to
ccol_dynmq_enable_sending(). A send that the queue refuses keeps the
ownership of its own msg->data.

A dynamic queue grows on demand, so no sender parks and waits for room. A
circular queue is different. There is therefore no broadcast to blocked
senders here. The function does wake every write-direction waiter on this
queue. Such a waiter is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call,
or an ccol_event_loop registration. Each of them scans again and sees the
new state. It therefore does not wait for a send that the queue no longer
permits.

A receive does not change. The messages that are already in the queue stay
there, and a receive still gets them in the normal way. This is what makes
this function the natural way to close a producer side and let a consumer
drain what is left.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue that gets sends turned off
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - The function turned sends off
ccol_invalid_args  - dq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ccol_dynmq_enable_sending() turns sends on again.
The function does not change a receive.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_enable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_msg_count (3),
.BR ccol_circq_disable_sending (3)
